WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.040
Film Courage: What is it like as a director
trying to make your day?

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.809
By the hour, minute.

00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:12.560
Jay Silverman, Photographer/Director/Producer:
Number one, I’m the executive producer of

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.270
this movie, I’m also the director and I
think it’s so critical that boundaries are

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:28.160
set right away and in the case of Will Newman
(my producer) he personally was responsible

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.870
in advance for scheduling may day in addition
to my DP.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:40.770
And Matt Edwards who is the DP of the movie
is really the most important person on the

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:48.780
set because if he slows down or can’t make
the day he previously agreed to this has an

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:58.620
incredibly dark story and at the end of the
day the next day you have a whole other and

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:10.290
what Will made such an 
asset is your ability to be honest with yourself.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:17.540
And I mentioned earlier that the screenwriter
had a beautiful third act where hot air balloons

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.370
where being chased by a truck and actors were
hanging out of the truck and hanging out of

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:30.520
the balloon and there is just no way we could
have done that in even an economical way versus

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:36.560
the way we ended up doing in which was less
than half of a day on the Rio Grande.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.350
That was our creative solution that the screenwriter
agreed to.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:48.270
If she didn’t agree to it we would have
come up with something else.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:55.450
And as a producer and a director it was my
goal to make the goal everyday and I think

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:02.320
we made the day everyday but one day and the
reason we didn’t make it that day was something

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.440
that was unexpected happens and it happens
on the time in the business.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:15.380
I mean one of the real tragedies of doing
independent filmmaking is the things you can’t

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.380
control.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.090
So my movie takes place predominantly in Taos,
New Mexico and in LA.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:32.069
And I made a significantly smart decision
to build a kitchen here on my stage and we

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.510
could flick the switch each day and modify
the lighting and never have to worry about

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:41.800
the sun going behind the building next door
and never having to worry about going into

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:47.410
overtime and no light at all.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:56.220
It gave us the ability to do two times more
work than we would have New Mexico in a real

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.590
kitchen.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:03.190
And most importantly creatively I was able
to work with a set designer I was able to

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:09.320
create something that I felt was the most
magical parts of the movie was her domain,

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.750
the restaurant, her dining room.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.280
And all that was made on the stage.

